Handle XRef::copy() returning nullptr in FontInfoScanner::scan() and
Page::displaySlice().

XRef::copy() returns nullptr when it cannot allocate the entry table (it already
logs "unable to allocate {0:d} entries"), but both callers used the result
unchecked. FontInfoScanner::scan() then passed a null XRef to scanFonts(), and
XRef::fetch() ran on a null `this` and reconstructed the "xref" from address 0;
Page::displaySlice() (copyXRef=true, used by the cpp page renderer) handed it to
replaceXRef() and createGfx(). Under UBSan with a heap cap (LD_PRELOAD shim):
"FontInfo.cc:184:28: runtime error: member call on null pointer of type 'XRef'".
On wasm32 the guest hits its memory cap copying a ~16M-entry table built from
object numbers like 8388609, and the null fetch ends in an indirect call through
garbage (Abort(IndirectCallTypeMismatch) in XRef::constructXRef).

The qt5/qt6 bindings have two more unchecked XRef::copy() calls (not built here).

Regression test cpp/tests/poppler-xref-copy-failure-test.cpp loads a document
with a 2M-entry xref table, caps the address space (RLIMIT_AS) so that copying
the table fails, and scans its fonts (a segfault without the fix). It is
skipped under AddressSanitizer.

Not fixed upstream as of 9182fd5.
Upstream: none found (poppler/poppler issues and MRs searched for XRef::copy,
FontInfoScanner); merged !2327 (2026-08-08, in the pin) made XRef::copy()
return a unique_ptr and left the callers unchecked.

